### Rationale

The same abstract state-transition diagram may not preserve the same causal capacities when implemented in materials with different:

* timescales;
* noise properties;
* energetic requirements;
* nonlinearities;
* susceptibility to perturbation;
* boundary-forming mechanisms;
* memory effects;
* coupling to environmental and bodily processes.

The theory therefore rejects unrestricted substrate neutrality while remaining compatible with limited multiple realization. Two materially different systems may instantiate the same conscious organization only if they preserve all psychophysically relevant counterfactual relations.

