## 3. Empirical basis for material enactment

### 3.1 Biointerfacial water is physically distinguishable

Recent Raman microspectroscopy of living cells found that intracellular water is predominantly bulk-like but contains a consistent minority population of non-bulk-like water near biomolecules. This biointerfacial water represented approximately (3%) of intracellular water in the studied cells and displayed a weakened and more disordered tetrahedral hydrogen-bond structure. The authors estimated roughly one molecular hydration layer around soluble proteins.

This establishes:

[
W_{\mathrm{cell}}
=================

W_{\mathrm{bulk-like}}
+
W_{\mathrm{interface}}.
]

It does not establish a body-wide coherent phase.

### 3.2 Hydration and macromolecular function are dynamically coupled

Experimental neutron-scattering and simulation studies show that hydration-water translational dynamics correlate with large-amplitude functional motions in both folded and intrinsically disordered proteins. Biological activity is generally lost when proteins are hydrated below the level required for their dynamical transition.

The relevant relation is reciprocal:

[
\dot P
======

f(P,W),
\qquad
\dot W
======

g(W,P).
]

Water is neither an inert environment nor an autonomous controller. Protein and hydration dynamics form a coupled system.

### 3.3 Water participates in ion-channel dynamics

Ion-channel operation depends on ion hydration, pore hydration and water permeability. TRPV1 research found a direct relation between water migration, cation flow and channel functionality; replacing ordinary water with deuterated water altered channel activity and downstream nociceptive behavior.

This demonstrates a causal pathway of the form:

[
W
\rightarrow
P_{\mathrm{channel}}
\rightarrow
I_{\mathrm{ion}}
\rightarrow
B_{\mathrm{neural}}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}.
]

It does not show that water independently carries phenomenal content.

### 3.4 Hydration changes extracellular mechanics

The extracellular matrix is not a dry scaffold. Water and ions contribute to its swelling, elasticity, stress relaxation and coupling to cells. Changes in loosely bound water have been associated with changes in ECM mechanical behavior, while ECM organization itself plays an active role in morphogenesis.

Therefore:

[
W_i
\rightarrow
E_i
\rightarrow
C_i
\rightarrow
\text{cell behavior}.
]

The material substrate is electrochemical and mechanical at once.

### 3.5 Bioelectric organization regulates form

Developmental bioelectricity studies show that resting membrane potentials arise from ionic concentrations, ion-channel states and coupling through gap junctions. These electrical variables affect gene expression, cellular behavior, tissue polarity, regeneration and anatomical pattern.

Temporary perturbation of planarian bioelectric networks can produce altered regenerative anatomy that persists through later rounds of regeneration, supporting the interpretation that bioelectric state contributes to morphogenetic memory.

The cautious conclusion is:

[
\boxed{
\text{bioelectric organization is a candidate carrier of}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{morphogenetic state information.}
}
]

It has not been shown to be the complete storage substrate.

