## 6. History as material disposition

The investigation of hydration and bioelectricity sharpens the meaning of history dependence.

The process’s history is not an immaterial record attached to its present state. It is present as altered disposition:

[
H_i(t)
======

\left{
z\in x_i(t):
z\text{ depends on the previous trajectory and changes future dynamics}
\right}.
]

Such dispositions may include:

* synaptic weights;
* ion distributions;
* channel expression;
* gap-junction topology;
* protein conformations;
* cytoskeletal prestress;
* extracellular remodeling;
* hydration-shell dynamics;
* metabolic states;
* epigenetic states.

The causal effect of history is:

[
P(x_{t+\Delta}\mid x_t,H_t)
\neq
P(x_{t+\Delta}\mid x_t).
]

The theory therefore identifies the bearer with an actively retained trajectory rather than an instantaneous arrangement.

