## 11. Falsification conditions

The Hydrated Bioelectric Contribution Hypothesis would be weakened if:

1. hydration variables fail to alter relevant bioelectric dynamics after confounds are controlled;
2. apparent hydration effects are fully mediated by known osmotic, metabolic or mechanical variables;
3. morphogenetic memory persists unchanged under interventions that disrupt the proposed hydration variables while preserving other physiology;
4. hydration variables add no predictive value to conscious-state models;
5. hydration-informed boundaries diverge from neural, physiological and behavioral subject boundaries;
6. effects observed in simplified membranes or isolated proteins fail to generalize to living tissue.

The stronger claim that hydration is constitutive of consciousness would require evidence that selective disruption of hydration-dependent dynamics abolishes or transforms consciousness while preserving all competing candidate mechanisms. No such evidence presently exists.

