## 12. Relation to Abhinavagupta

This section is comparative philosophy, not scientific evidence.

Abhinavagupta’s system does not present consciousness as an output manufactured by inert matter. Contemporary interpretation characterizes his view as a panentheist or dual-aspect account in which subjectivity is pervasive within material reality and matter is not wholly external to consciousness.

Within that framework, the material organization of a body explains the **specific contraction and expression** of a finite subject, not the existence of manifestness itself.

The relevant distinction is:

[
\text{condition of finite expression}
\neq
\text{source of manifestation}.
]

Hydration, bioelectric organization, metabolism and embodiment would belong to the conditions through which a finite organism acquires:

* this sensorium;
* this memory;
* this action repertoire;
* this boundary;
* this affective profile.

They would not explain why anything is manifest.

Abhinavagupta’s concept of *vimarśa* has been interpreted as active self-articulation: consciousness is not passive illumination but possesses the capacity to differentiate and act in material reality.

A historically cautious comparative formulation is:

> Material organization specifies how a finite perspective is enacted; self-manifestation specifies why the process is intrinsically present rather than merely externally describable.

The minimal scientific theory does not adopt Abhinavagupta’s universal-consciousness ontology. That interpretation can be presented as a metaphysical extension:

[
A_i[\Sigma_i]
=============

\mathcal C!\restriction_{L_i},
]

where (\mathcal C) is universal self-manifesting reality and (L_i) is the materially realized constraint structure defining finite perspective.

This extension is philosophically compatible with the revised theory but is not empirically entailed by it.

