## 15. Final theory statement

> **Materially Enacted Inexternalist Process Identity Theory holds that a conscious subject is numerically identical to one temporally extended, history-dependent, materially enacted process-equivalence class. The bearer is neither an abstract computation nor a fixed material inventory. It is a metastable organization whose neural, bioelectric, autonomic, metabolic, mechanical, extracellular and sensorimotor capacities reciprocally preserve one trajectory. Hydration and interfacial-water dynamics are proposed as constitutive contributors insofar as they modulate protein function, ion transport, membrane behavior, extracellular mechanics and bioelectric coupling. This material contribution is empirically testable but does not itself explain phenomenality or individuate the subject. Subject individuation requires convergence among independently recovered physical boundaries, while psychophysical identity requires systematic mappings between the organization of the material process and independently characterized phenomenal unity, temporality, boundary, quality, valence and agency.**

